    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-assistant-6010464?source=google.com": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Assistant Research Assistant Back to jobs Research Assistant Job details Posted 28 August 2026 Salary Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata End date 06 September 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 206893 Expiry 06 Sep 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to the ESRC Impact Acceleration Account-funded project Navigating Political Change in Local Government, working with the Principal Investigator, Dr Fred Paxton, and the project's partner organisation. The project provides structured, research-informed guidance to help senior local authority officers navigate the political transitions arising from recent changes in local government composition, including the election of Reform UK councillors and gains by the Greens and local independents across multiple authorities in 2025 and 2026. It draws on the Principal Investigator's comparative research on populist radical right parties in local government. You will provide research and coordination support across all three work packages: the practitioner working groups, the delivery and evaluation of pilot workshops with member councils, and the finalisation and dissemination of professionally produced training materials. The role requires strong organisational skills, clear written communication, and the ability to coordinate effectively with senior practitioners and an external partner organisation. Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with and under the guidance of the Principal/Co Investigator: 1.  Support logistics for practitioner working group sessions, including scheduling, platform coordination, and participant communications, and assist the Principal Investigator with session preparation. 2.  Produce written summaries of key themes and emerging lessons from working group sessions. 3.  Coordinate the circulation of draft training materials to working group members and collate written feedback. 4.  Design and distribute participant feedback questionnaires; collect, analyse, and report on responses. 5.  Assist in coordinating pilot workshop delivery for member councils, including preparation of supporting materials and facilitator briefing notes. 6.  Produce a short written feedback report summarising pilot findings and recommendations for revision. 7.  Support the revision and finalisation of training materials, liaising with graphic designers on production logistics and coordinating review cycles. 8.  Administer post-dissemination evaluation surveys and assist with analysis of follow-up responses. 9.  Support the development of the ESRC IAA impact case study in collaboration with the Principal Investigator and the College Research Support Office (CRSO). Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 SCQF Level 10 (Honours degree) in a relevant subject or a cognate discipline, or equivalent A2 Specialist theoretical and practical relevant to the project, including UK local government politics and policy and the dynamics of party political change in local authorities A3 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of the wider subject area or subject specialism Desirable: B1 A postgraduate degree (taught or research) in politics, public policy, public administration, or a closely related social science discipline — either currently enrolled or recently completed Skills Essential: C1 Project-specific skills: excellent organisational and coordination skills, including the ability to manage scheduling, participant communications, and event logistics for online and in-person sessions with senior practitioners; and the ability to produce clear, well-structured written outputs such as summaries, briefing notes, and reports C2 Research creativity and cross-discipline collaborative ability as appropriate C3 Excellent communication skills (oral and written), including public presentations and ability to communicate complex data/concepts clearly and concisely C4 Excellent interpersonal skills including team working and a collegiate approach C5 Appropriate workload/time/project/budget/people management skills C6 Extensive IT and data analysis/interpretation skills as appropriate C7 Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought/working C8 Problem solving skills including a flexible and pragmatic approach Desirable: D1 Knowledge of UK local government structures, politics, or policy processes D2 Familiarity with the research underpinning the project (populist and radical right parties, and political transitions in local government) Experience Essential: E1 Sufficient relevant research experience (or equivalent) appropriate to an early career researcher E2 Project-specific experience: experience of coordinating events, meetings, or research activities, and of producing written outputs (summaries, briefing notes, or reports) for a professional or academic audience E3 Experience of scientific writing and research E4 Proven ability to deliver quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner E5 Evidence of an emerging track record of high quality research publications in a relevant field Desirable: F1 Experience of conducting or supporting qualitative or mixed-methods research (e.g. interviews, focus groups, surveys) F2 Experience of designing or analysing questionnaire-based surveys F3 Experience of stakeholder engagement or professional communications in a research or public sector context Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata. This post is offered on a fractional basis (155 hours over duration of contract) and is fixed term until 30 April 2027. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date:  6 September 2026 at 23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Assistant Glasgow Posted 6 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Barista Glasgow £13.45 per hour Support Administrator Glasgow Grade 3, £24,685 - £25,249 per annum Clinician in Anaesthesia Glasgow Grade 7/8: £41,064 - £46,049/£50,253 - £58,225 per annum pro rata Student Ambassador Glasgow £24,685 pa -  £25,804 pa Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
