    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-6008480?source=google.com": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate Research Associate Back to jobs Research Associate Job details Posted 27 August 2026 Salary Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum End date 10 September 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 206954 Expiry 10 Sep 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose The postholder will undertake and coordinate laboratory-based investigations examining the molecular and biological effects of childhood pubertal suppression using banked ovine tissues. The postholder will join a multidisciplinary translational research programme spanning epidemiology, clinical medicine and comparative physiology. The post offers a unique opportunity to generate novel mechanistic data relating to biological ageing, inflammation, oxidative stress, metabolic signalling and fibrosis The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Take a leading role in the planning and conduct of assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and College research strategy. 2. Undertake laboratory-based analyses and perform molecular biology techniques including nucleic acid extraction, gene expression analyses, histological assessments and DNA methylation studies. 3. Maintain accurate laboratory records, study databases and sample inventories in accordance with University policies and good research practice. 4. Analyse and interpret experimental data, including investigation of treatment effects, sex differences and treatment-sex interactions. 5. Work collaboratively with investigators from cardiovascular sciences, paediatric endocrinology, veterinary medicine and physiology to integrate mechanistic findings with clinical and epidemiological work packages. 6. Contribute to the preparation of high-quality manuscripts, conference abstracts, presentations and research reports arising from the project 7. Present research findings at local, national and international scientific meetings 8. Establish and maintain your research profile and reputation and that of the University of Glasgow, including establishing and sustaining a track record of independent and joint publications of international quality, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 9. Survey the research literature and environment, understand the research challenges associated with the project & subject area, and develop/implement a suitable research strategy to deliver project/area requirements and opportunities. 10. Take a leading role in the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research and impact. 11. Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across the research group/area/School/College/University and wider community (e.g. academic and industrial partners). 12. Take a leading role in team/group meetings/seminars/workshops and School research group/area activities to enhance the wider knowledge, outputs and culture of the School/College. 13. Take the lead in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 14. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 15. Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 16. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by your line manager and the Head of School. 17. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with University strategy. Knowledge, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment, or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience in physiology, cardiovascular science, molecular biology, biomedical sciences, endocrinology, neuroscience or a closely related discipline. A2 Knowledge of mechanisms involved in cardiovascular biology, developmental physiology, endocrinology or neurobiology. Desirable B1  Knowledge of mechanisms relating to inflammation, oxidative stress, fibrosis or metabolic signalling. Skills C1 Evidence of scientific writing and dissemination through publications, presentations or project reports. C2 Excellent organisational, project management and time management skills. C3 Ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team. C4 Strong written and verbal communication skills. C5 Commitment to open and reproducible research practices Experience Essential E1 Demonstrated experience of laboratory-based biomedical research. E2 Experience in the analysis and interpretation of biological datasets. E3 Experience maintaining accurate laboratory records and managing research data. Desirable F1 Experience of histology, immunohistochemistry or tissue staining techniques. F2 Experience working with animal tissues or translational animal models. F3 Experience using statistical software such as R, SPSS, GraphPad Prism or equivalent. F4 Experience supervising students or junior researchers. F5 Experience preparing research funding applications. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week), and has funding available until 31 July 2028 in the first instance. The University of Glasgow is committed to safeguarding children and protected adults by ensuring that individuals in regulated roles are appropriately vetted and monitored. The University of Glasgow’s PVG policy aligns with the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020 and reflects the legal requirements effective from 1 April 2025, including the mandatory PVG Scheme membership for regulated roles. The post for which you are applying is considered to be a regulated role under the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020. Under the Act, individuals working in a regulated role must be a PVG Scheme member before employment. If you are successful in your application, the offer of employment will be conditional subject to a satisfactory PVG Scheme record check. We will be in touch regarding requirements related to this. Further information is available at: https://www.mygov.scot/pvg-scheme The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  10 September 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Associate Glasgow Posted 7 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Organisational Development Partner Glasgow Grade 8, £50,253 - £58,225 per annum Barista Glasgow £13.45 per hour Support Administrator Glasgow Grade 3, £24,685 - £25,249 per annum Clinician in Anaesthesia Glasgow Grade 7/8: £41,064 - £46,049/£50,253 - £58,225 per annum pro rata Student Ambassador Glasgow £24,685 pa -  £25,804 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University 