    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-scientist-scottish-marine-animal-stranding-scheme-smass-6028530?source=google.com": {
      "title": "Research Scientist",
      "description": "Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Back to jobs Featured Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Job details Posted 14 September 2026 Salary Grade 6 £33,951 - £37,694 Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 207133 Expiry 12 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To undertake high-quality research within the University of Glasgow’s Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS), one of the longest-running marine mammal disease surveillance systems in the world. For over thirty years SMASS has investigated the health and mortality of whales, dolphins, seals and other marine animals around Scotland’s coast, and the postholder would become an integral part of that work. Working with the Principal Investigator, the postholder will apply specialist scientific knowledge and independent investigation to the capture, structuring, visualisation and analysis of marine mammal strandings and pathology data, making a leading contribution to the scheme’s research outputs, publications, funding applications and collaborative activities. At Grade 7, the postholder will apply advanced specialist expertise to develop research methodologies, demonstrate a growing external research profile, and contribute to research leadership, supervision and wider academic activities. Role Overview The postholder will support two complementary strands of work. The first is operational and analytical: leading the maintenance and continued development of the SMASS strandings database, necropsy portal and sample archives, overseeing the BeachTrack mobile application and its supporting data infrastructure, and producing the analysis, visualisations and outputs required for reporting, outreach and communication. The second is providing technical input to the LIFE CIBBRiNA project, scoping a harmonised, web-accessible database for sharing marine mammal and ETP (Endangered, Threatened and Protected) species strandings and bycatch data, in collaboration with the International Whaling Commission (IWC), ASCOBANS and European partners. Beyond this core remit, the post offers scope to engage with the full breadth of SMASS activities, including strandings coordination and response, post-mortem examination, volunteer support, knowledge exchange and outreach. It will suit someone with a genuine interest in marine mammal pathology and the fieldwork that underpins it, and will include attendance at strandings and post-mortems. Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with, and under the guidance of, the Principal Investigator: Database management and development 1. Take a leading role in maintaining and developing the centralised relational SMASS database and necropsy portal, including oversight of the BeachTrack data infrastructure; lead and document updates and new features, working with the University of Glasgow Software Services (GUSS) development team to implement them. 2. Develop and embed pathological attributes within the database, applying an understanding of marine mammal pathophysiology to capture necropsy findings for curation, analysis and reporting. 3. Set and uphold data-entry standards, and undertake validation, quality assurance and curation of both new records and the historical strandings archive; harmonise and format data for statutory and international reporting, resolving data inconsistencies and maintaining consistent documentation and metadata throughout. 4. Contribute to the pathological and epidemiological coding and data entry of necropsy reports, and develop improved metrics and methodologies for diagnosing bycatch from pathology data. Data analysis, interpretation and visualisation 5. Undertake statistical, temporal and spatial analysis of strandings data, including trend and time-series analysis. 6. Produce descriptive statistics, tables, figures and distribution maps, together with the analytical and interpretive content underpinning the quarterly and annual reports to government. 7. Develop tools, resources and outputs — including clear data visualisations and basic spatial and mapping (GIS) outputs — that support the exploration, interpretation and communication of the scheme’s scientific, statutory and communication needs. European data harmonisation (technical input and scoping) 8. Provide technical input to the scoping of a harmonised, web-accessible database for collating and assessing marine mammal and ETP species strandings and bycatch data at a European scale, defining the technical requirements to meet the monitoring needs of the relevant case studies. 9. Liaise closely with the IWC, which holds responsibility for delivery of the harmonised database, and advise on the data structures, harmonisation protocols and coding schemes required for consistency across national schemes and marine regions. 10. Coordinate with participating countries and assist regional organisations (such as the IWC, ICES and ASCOBANS) in the transition to, and active use of, a Global Strandings Database. Data requests, external data flows and statutory reporting 11. Handle external data and sample-access requests, providing extracts and summaries to researchers, collaborators, the Marine Directorate and partner organisations in line with institutional and data-protection requirements. 12. Maintain the public data and sample catalogue and the data dashboard, ensuring data integrity, accessibility and currency. 13. Compile, format and submit, in a timely manner, statutory and international data returns, including the data-sharing, reporting and compliance obligations owed to UK CSIP and to international bodies (MSFD, OSPAR, ICES, ASCOBANS and the IWC) under the relevant memoranda of understanding. 14. Support and train SMASS staff and volunteers in data entry and use of the database, communicating data processes and issues clearly to non-technical colleagues. Strandings response, volunteer support and operations 15. Participate in SMASS field strandings response, including the on-call coordination rota, and assist with necropsies, sample processing and archiving, including out-of-hours work in field and beach conditions. 16. Assist the SMASS volunteer training coordinator in delivering training courses for the volunteer network. 17. Provide administrative support to the SMASS programme, including the ordering and management of stock and consumable levels. 18. Develop and maintain digital content and tools to enhance the online presence and accessibility of the SMASS programme. Research, communication and professional development 19. Collaborate with internal and external partners to advance the research aims of SMASS and the broader strategic goals of the University of Glasgow and associated research networks, including contributing to publications, presentations, public engagement and the identification of future funding as opportunities allow. 20. Engage in continuing professional development, keep up to date with advances in the field, and contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with its strategic plan. 21. Undertake any other relevant duties, commensurate with the grade and nature of the post, as directed by the Principal Investigator. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: 22. Take a leading role in developing the underpinning research methodologies that deliver high-quality research deliverables aligned with the scheme’s and the School’s priorities, exercising a higher degree of independence, leadership and responsibility. 23. Take a leading role in translating research outputs and analytical findings into clear concepts, insights and key messages for diverse audiences, maintaining an active portfolio of communication outputs across appropriate digital platforms and engagement channels. 24. Keep abreast of emerging methods, technologies and infrastructure, advising senior colleagues on opportunities and gaps and recommending plans for growth and sustainability. 25. Establish and sustain a track record of independent and joint published research, contribute to grant applications and research proposals, and mentor colleagues and junior researchers. Deliverables 1. Reliable, well-documented delivery and maintenance of the SMASS strandings data and sample archive, with pathological attributes embedded and a suite of data visualisations and reporting outputs in regular use. 2. A scoping report for the European harmonised database, detailing the proposed architecture and data-sharing protocols developed as technical input to the IWC-led delivery. 3. Established and documented harmonised data-sharing protocols to inform the consortium’s adoption of the central database. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 SCQF level 10 (Honours degree) in a relevant subject or a cognate discipline (e.g. veterinary medicine, biological or marine science, or ecological data management), or an equivalent combination of relevant training and demonstrable practical experience — including, where relevant, a veterinary or medical qualification. A2 Demonstrable understanding of research design appropriate to the discipline, and familiarity with marine animal (or comparable) pathology and post-mortem examination — whether through a veterinary or medical background, formal training, or clear practical experience. Formal veterinary, medical or pathology training is not required, but applicants must be familiar with pathological material and nomenclature. A3 A genuine interest in marine mammal strandings and a willingness to participate in field response and necropsy. A4 Up-to-date knowledge of the legislative requirements applicable to the role, including but not limited to Health and Safety and data protection (GDPR). For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: A5 Postgraduate qualification (SCQF level 11, Master’s degree or equivalent professional experience); or the equivalent competencies acquired through significant relevant experience in strandings-related work in a similar or related role(s). A6 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within marine mammal pathology, strandings science or a cognate specialism. Desirable B1 An awarded (or recently submitted / near completion) PhD in a relevant scientific, technical or professional discipline. B2 Familiarity with the conservation status of marine species in the North-East Atlantic and with the technical challenges of bycatch estimation. B3 Working towards, or holding, professional registration with a relevant body (e.g. RCVS for veterinary applicants, or Chartered Scientist (CSci) / Registered Scientist (RSci) / MRSB via an appropriate professional body). Skills Essential C1 Strong attention to detail and accuracy, with a methodical, rigorous approach to recording, validating and structuring data. C2 Sound general IT skills, including proficiency in data-handling tools (e.g. Excel and shared storage systems such as OneDrive) and the aptitude to work with databases. C3 Clear written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate complex information clearly and concisely and to explain data processes and issues to non-technical colleagues. C4 A collaborative, patient and flexible approach to teamworking, including working with University of Glasgow programmers, project teams, researchers and external collaborators. C5 Ability to plan and prioritise a broad range of work, and to manage multiple datasets and tasks simultaneously to meet competing demands. C6 Proficiency in R programming language (or a comparable environment) for data handling, analysis and visualisation, or a strong aptitude and clear willingness to develop it. C7 Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought, with a flexible and pragmatic approach to problem solving. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: C8 Advanced specialist knowledge within marine mammal pathology, strandings analysis or database harmonisation, including relevant technical models, equipment or techniques. C9 Demonstrable expertise in conceptualising research projects and translating complex findings into clear, accessible outputs for both expert collaborators and non-specialist audiences, including through digital communication platforms. Desirable D1 Experience of data visualisation and of producing clear graphical and reporting outputs (e.g. ggplot2 or comparable tools), and of statistical, temporal or time-series analysis of ecological or comparable data. D2 Familiarity with relational databases and basic SQL, and with data formats and shared storage systems. D3 Basic GIS or spatial-data skills. D4 Experience in developing assessment toolkits for ETP (Endangered, Threatened, or Protected) species and in managing large-scale marine datasets. Experience Essential E1 Relevant work experience within a similar research, laboratory or field environment, including evidence of previous outputs and professional development. E2 Experience of data entry, validation and cleaning processes, and of maintaining documentation and metadata records. E3 Experience of operating independently in progressing and delivering goals within set timescales. E4 Experience of developing tools, resources, software or analytical outputs that support the interpretation, exploration or presentation of research findings. E5 Experience of contributing to public engagement, academic outreach and research publications. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: E6 Experience of working independently in a similar research environment to recommend and/or determine appropriate research methods, and of establishing and sustaining a track record of independent and joint published research. E7 Experience of supervising students or junior researchers, and of working on complex multi-partner projects. E8 A track record of presentation and publication of research results in quality journals and at conferences. E9 Experience of making a leading contribution in academic activities, and the ability to demonstrate independence — for example through the identification of project objectives from assessment of the literature, the design and analysis of investigations, and the drafting of papers. E10  Experience of undertaking independent research. Desirable F1 Experience of strandings response and/or necropsy, and of the strandings data workflow from the field through to analysis. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: F2 Experience of understanding and interpreting the technical requirements of service users and implementing appropriate methods of meeting their needs. F3 Experience of managing budgets or stock, including monitoring outgoings on behalf of others. F4 An emerging national or international reputation. For enquiries regarding this post please contact Dr Andrew Brownlow, Andrew.Brownlow@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6/7, £33,951 - £37,694 / £41,064 - £46,049 per annum This post is full time, and has funding for up to 15 months. ** The primary place of work for this role will be Glasgow. Flexible working arrangements may be considered for candidates based elsewhere in Scotland, subject to business requirements and the ability to attend and respond to stranding events as required. The University also has a base near Inverness, and consideration may be given to candidates wishing to be based there. However, applicants should be aware that the role will require regular travel to Glasgow. ** The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date: 12 October 2026 at 23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Glasgow Posted about 11 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951 - £37,694 Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 Reach Out Library Student Ambassador Glasgow Grade 3, £24,685 – £25,249 per annum Byres Community Hub Host Glasgow Grade 4, £25,804 - £27,319 per annum Lifelong Learning Tutor - Access Physics Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
