    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/project-manager-6022270?source=google.com": {
      "title": "Project Manager · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Project Manager · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Project Manager Project Manager Back to jobs Project Manager Job details Posted 09 September 2026 Salary £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 207513 Expiry 30 Sep 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To manage, coordinate and administer the portfolio of projects and activities of the Urban Big Data Centre (UBDC) and the ERC/ UKRI funded research project POLART. Both UBDC and POLART focus on the production of evidence for policy, albeit from two different, complementary angles: UBDC has been a dynamic national research hub for over 11 years, championing the use of new sources of smart data and evidence to inform policymaking and enhance the quality of urban life.  UBDC is currently undertaking a wide range of projects of varying sizes and from a range of funders, working with partners in the UK and internationally, including government, research councils, and the third sector, as well as policy and practice. POLART (‘Art and Policy in the Global Contemporary: examining the role of the arts in the production of public policy’) is a 5 year research project that brings together an interdisciplinary research team of  art, humanities and social science researchers to explore visual and performing arts as a rich field of alternative sources of expert knowledge for policy making. The role supports and advises the Centre's Director and POLART PI and project team to ensure the effective and efficient delivery of objectives, projects and activities, which encompasses oversight of a complex multi-institutional research and knowledge exchange programme; management of a combined +£5m budget; and the coordination and delivery of a range of projects, events and other initiatives. The role reports to the College Research Operations Manager as part of the College team supporting Centres and Projects.  Workload will be assigned via the Centre Director and POLART PI. Main Duties and Responsibilities Lead and manage the Centre’s/ POLART’s operational and administrative affairs, including ensuring the effective delivery and management of a range of research projects and milestones. Working closely with the UBDC Director/Deputy Director  and POLART PI to co-ordinate and respond to external funding opportunities, including supporting budget development, approvals and submissions. Supervision and workload allocation and line management of a small group of administrative staff providing support to research projects at the Centre. As a member of the Centre Management/ POLART team, provide advice and support across all aspects of the Centre’s/ POLART operational activities, including supporting the development of income generation opportunities. Working with College Research Support Office to manage, disburse, monitor and report on the Centre’s/POLART’s budget and resources, including co-ordinating with partners and funders regarding invoicing. Plan, coordinate, manage and monitor the Centre’s/POLART’s work-streams and portfolio of projects and initiatives, including liaising with external partners. Oversee the Centre’s/POLART’s programme of seminars, conferences, training events and other events. Manage the logistics of the workshops, coordinating the facilitators and managing all data and materials that are required or generated during these events. Oversee the Centre’s/POLART’s external and internal communications, working closely with the Communications & Engagement colleagues at the College of Social Sciences. Lead on the development and delivery of a Communication Strategy, for example setting up and maintain POLART’s web and social media presence producing briefings, blogs, podcasts, explainer videos and similar material, and providing promotional content to other channels such as the University’s Marketing teams, as required by the research team. Manage the Centre’s/POLART’s internal information-sharing and data management systems and procedures. Establish and sustain project management processes and key project documents (e.g. report writing) to ensure a record of contacts, events, outputs, and other project activities are kept up-to-date, accessible and accurate. Act as the principal administrative liaison point for the Centre/POLART with its key funders and stakeholders, leading on the provision of information about and reporting on the Centre’s activities and operations. Undertake any other relevant duties as directed by the Centre Director, POLART PI or Head of College Operations. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 8 (Scottish Vocational Qualification level 4, Higher National Diploma) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role, or ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar related role(s). A2 Thorough understanding and knowledge of programme and project management methodologies. Desirable B1 Formal project management qualification (s) (SCRUM Master, PRINCE2 Practitioner; MSP Practitioner certificate or similar). B2 Familiarity with the challenges of working within a complex, multi-partner research project. B3 Knowledge and experience of the challenges and opportunities of working on equitable international collaborations. B4 Some familiarity with research/ policy relationship, as well as familiarity with cultural policy and arts sector contexts. Skills Essential C1 Planning, management and delivery of a portfolio of projects and initiatives. C2 Experience of setting, manging and monitoring budgets and resources. C3 Excellent qualitative and quantitative analytical skills. C4 A very high level of personal initiative with the ability to work proactively and often autonomously . C5 Proven ability to develop effective working relationships with colleagues and external partners. C6 Experience of website content management and social media communications. C7 Ability to multi-task, assess priorities and work flexibly according to changing priorities. C8 Proven ability to produce reports, presentations and technical document for a variety of audiences. C9Excellent attention to detail and to the production of accurate, high-quality outputs. C10 Excellent digital and IT skills, including use of project management, collaboration and communication/design tools and software. C11 Proven ability to liaise and cooperate across organisational and geographical boundaries. C12 Leadership and motivational skills. C13 Effective negotiation and diplomacy skills. C14 Ability to manage confidentiality and sensitive information appropriately. Experience Essential E1 In depth experience of successfully managing and delivering projects in the Higher Education or relevant experience. E2 Experience of supervising administrative staff. E3 Experience of using corporate financial and administrative business systems, including experience of research council finance systems E4 Experience of budgetary management and monitoring and ability to monitor budget and produce financial reporting as per the requirements of the funder and the university E5 Experience of managing project deliverables, KPIs, and writing reports. E6 Experience of all aspects of research communication and events organisation. E7 Experience of dealing with processes and procedures for partnership working and public engagement. Desirable F1 Experience of line managing administrative staff. F2 Experience of research administration in the Higher Education sector and/or of supporting the development of public policy in local, regional or national government body or similar. F3 Experience with using software to support collaborative work and project management (e.g., MS teams, MS project, Trello, Miro, GitHub). F4 Experience of workshop facilitation. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum pro rata. This post is part time (35 hours per week) and fixed term for up till the 31.10.2028 Closing date: 23:45 30th September 2026 The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Project Manager Glasgow Posted 6 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951 - £37,694 Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 Reach Out Library Student Ambassador Glasgow Grade 3, £24,685 – £25,249 per annum Byres Community Hub Host Glasgow Grade 4, £25,804 - £27,319 per annum Lifelong Learning Tutor - Access Physics Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
